] [17592186152483 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.$84 \\cdot 102mod5 =$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b81b6d2b-abd2-5154-9eb2-356526df808d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152486 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $19^{19}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73829fe7-6343-5529-8a3e-7f6982fe6b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152949 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152949 #edb/ref 1061 154615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152949 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "150a8841-cd4a-55a0-9103-03b339daec90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152949 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152950 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152950 #edb/ref 1061 154616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152950 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb1b2b70-7fdf-523c-bc68-d9bd60ecf54c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152950 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152951 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152951 #edb/ref 1061 154619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25406990-f673-5af1-86df-1fa7639c14ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152952 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152952 #edb/ref 1061 154620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152952 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c24bd18-6dc6-5fee-9d4f-4f05d01d4776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152952 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152953 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152953 #edb/ref 1061 154623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152953 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4cc2be4-a3b1-5aa1-8676-c62d501d2546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152953 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152954 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152954 #edb/ref 1061 154627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152954 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "deefc65a-57e3-571f-a62b-93fbf75d0ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152954 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152955 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152955 #edb/ref 1061 154628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152955 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aaa53ec0-7f0e-5c35-81f0-63d71abae912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152955 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152956 #edb/ref 1061 154631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152956 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82d98a08-ff41-586d-97b2-d5622638b737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152956 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153451 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 7 & ≡9 \\cdot 2 & (mod5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5$.\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$,\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153451 #edb/ref 1065 9017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153451 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a3e0278c-44da-546e-bc47-9529380f4b5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153451 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153463 #edb/ref 1064 "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153463 #edb/ref 1065 9040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153463 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5778b7fb-626e-59d3-ad81-8d574bb2b4e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153463 #edb/ref 1067 "The Multiplicative Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153469 #edb/ref 1064 "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$. By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain\n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153469 #edb/ref 1065 9076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153469 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5dad198-84e6-5eab-9d7e-6df354768fce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153469 #edb/ref 1067 "The Exponentiation Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153473 #edb/ref 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟹a - b = pn; ⟹k(a - b) = kpn; ⟹ka - kb = (kp)n\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nkp = q\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nka - kb = (kp)n \\mid ⟹ka - kb = qn; ⟹ka≡kb(modn)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153473 #edb/ref 1065 9125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5cf4e7b1-34e6-55ea-bb8f-ae13a8fa29d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153473 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving the Scalar Multiplication Property" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
